racts in the project, they're all for pkg/ packages—main packages don't have meaningful contracts since they have no exports. The SRD itself serves as the contract for cmd/ packages, and the existing tasks skip contracts for cmd/ utilities entirely. I'll follow that established pattern.\n\nNow I need to verify I'm not exceeding the 4-task limit and focusing on the earliest incomplete release. Looking at rel10.1, chown has ready requirements, but chmod and chgrp aren't even listed in requirement_states, suggesting they're either already complete or not in ready state. Let me check what's actually available to propose. I can fit 2 more tasks before moving to rel11.0, so I'll start with mkfifo since it comes first alphabetically. My task breakdown is clean: chown split into two tasks handling R2 and R3 requirements respectively, then mkfifo's R1 requirements followed by its R2 requirements, with proper dependency ordering. Now I need to write out the descriptions including deliverables for each task.\n\nWriting task specifications... I'm second-guessing whether to keep the requirements generic or add more specifics. The generic approach is cleaner since the stitch agent can read the SRD directly, but it loses helpful context about what R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3 actually cover for chown. I think I'll stick with the generic version—it's unambiguous and lets the agent handle the details. I'm laying out the task structure with the SRD reference, file modifications needed, and specific requirements tied to the implementation sections. Now I'm defining the design decisions that will guide the implementation approach. I'm catching myself inventing design decisions that aren't grounded in the SRD or architecture. I need to stick only to what's actually specified—the SIGPIPE handler pattern and the differential testing approach with gchown as the reference binary—rather than prescribing implementation details like filepath.WalkDir.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I should focus on verifiable outcomes: the build succeeds, linting passes, and the differential tests run against gchown. Since I can't read the SRD directly, I'll let the stitch agent discover additional constraints when it processes the full specification.\n\nFor the mkfifo task, the stitch agent will need to create both the main binary and its test file following the same differential testing pattern, and I should clarify whether the existing chown_test.go needs modifications or if a new test file is required. I'm realizing the differential tests in the test file are actually the verification mechanism for this project, not separate unit tests—they're part of the implementation deliverable itself, which matches how the previous tasks were structured. I'll include the test file as part of the implementation task and move forward with the final plan.\n\nNow I'm checking the weight constraints and sub-requirement counts across all four tasks to make sure everything fits within the limits, then I'll generate the YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46174,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":46174},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3365f9f-47a9-4743-9439-04b9d1ee018b","uuid":"cb865642-6dfb-4369-a9ff-2414c87c7241"}
